cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Presence and Content Cancel: A Matched-Placebo Instrument for Ablating Agent State Components
message: >-
  If you use this software or the released databases, please cite them using
  the metadata in this file.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Uday
    family-names: Gattu
    email: udaygattu007@gmail.com
repository-code: 'https://github.com/UdayGattu/comprehension-defection'
url: 'https://github.com/UdayGattu/comprehension-defection'
abstract: >-
  Agent frameworks license a memory or state component by ablation: run the
  agent with the component, run it without, report the difference. That contrast
  is a sum of two channels - the presence and form of the component, and the
  content it carries - and the two need not agree in sign, so a null contrast
  does not identify an inert component. A token- and density-matched placebo arm
  bounds each channel separately. In one 7B agent the two channels are +0.1934
  and -0.2136 in episode-level defection rate, summing to the -0.0202 an
  ordinary ablation would report and read as inert. Eight experiments over three
  open-weight instruct models (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct,
  Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3), two readouts, three label framings and up to six
  arms establish what a valid component ablation requires: parity on token IDs
  rather than characters, an enforced content-density floor, and per-field
  falsification verified by self-join against recorded ground truth. Each is
  stated because this project's own pipeline violated it. A pre-registered test
  of whether the repaired estimate transports across serialisation template,
  field order and insertion position rejects additivity in six of six model x
  opponent groups. The release includes the pre-registrations, every
  experiment's SQLite database, and the analysis scripts that regenerate every
  quoted interval.
keywords:
  - large language models
  - llm agents
  - ablation
  - causal identification
  - matched placebo
  - iterated prisoner's dilemma
  - game theory
  - state tracking
  - comprehension
  - placebo control
  - pre-registration
  - reproducibility
  - Llama
  - Qwen
  - Mistral
